Steady State
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec:steadyState}

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Steady state behaviour vs the transient dynamics.
 
\layout Itemize

What do we mean by steady state? 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Enumerate

A stable condition that does not change over time or in which change in
 one direction is continually balanced by change in another.
\begin_inset Foot
collapsed false

\layout Standard


\family typewriter 
http://www.dictionary.com
\family default 
.
\end_inset 


\layout Enumerate

A steady state is a dynamic equilibrium.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout Itemize

A Markov process 
\emph on 
can
\emph default 
 reach a steady state distribtuion.
 
\layout Itemize

A probabilistic process 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(k|t\right)$
\end_inset 

 is a `noisy function' of time.
 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Itemize

If process converges to a distribution as time 
\begin_inset Formula $\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

 then it has a steady state.
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}p\left(k|t\right)=p\left(k\right)\]

\end_inset 

the average value of 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 at steady state is then given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle k\right\rangle _{p\left(k\right)}=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}p\left(k\right)k.\]

\end_inset 


\end_deeper 
\layout Itemize

A Poisson doesn't have a steady state distribution.
