\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:flowAtBoundaryMMinf}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

From the flow rates at the boundary we have in general
\layout Itemize


\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda_{k-1}P\left(k-1\right)=\mu_{k}P\left(k\right)\]

\end_inset 


\begin_deeper 
\layout Itemize

There is infinite service capacity in the queue the service rate at state
 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 is simply 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}=k\mu$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

The rate of arrivals stays constant, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{k}=\lambda$
\end_inset 

, so for this queue detailed balance gives.
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda P\left(k-1\right)=k\mu P\left(k\right)\]

\end_inset 


\end_deeper 
\layout Itemize

Reorganising in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(k\right)$
\end_inset 

 then gives
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\lambda}{k\mu}P\left(k-1\right)\]

\end_inset 

and therefore
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=P\left(0\right)\left(\frac{\lambda}{\mu}\right)^{k}\frac{1}{k!}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Normalisation Condition
\layout Itemize

Using the normalisation condition for this queue, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}P\left(k\right)=1,\]

\end_inset 

we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(0\right)$
\end_inset 

 as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P\left(0\right)=\left[\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left(\frac{\lambda}{\mu}\right)^{k}\frac{1}{k!}\right]^{-1}.\label{eq:MMinftyP0}\end{equation}

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

The Offered Load
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sub:offeredLoad}

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

It is convenient to define the offered load, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
a=\frac{\lambda}{\mu}\]

\end_inset 

as arrival rate divided by service rate.
 
\layout Itemize

This is a dimensionless constant, but has international standard units of
 Erlangs (after A.\SpecialChar ~
K.\SpecialChar ~
Erlang).
 
\layout Itemize

Substituting this definition back into (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:MMinftyP0}

\end_inset 

) we have, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)=\left[\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{a^{k}}{k!}\right]^{-1}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Recalling that 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^{n}}{n!}=\exp\left(x\right)\]

\end_inset 

 we have
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)=\exp\left(-a\right)\]

\end_inset 

and the steady state distribution is then
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{a^{k}}{k!}\exp\left(-a\right)\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Which is recognised as a Poisson distribution with parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Utilization Factor
\layout Itemize

Notice that the utilization factor for this queue, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset 

, is 
\emph on 
not
\emph default 
 equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\lambda}{\mu}$
\end_inset 

 which is refered to as the offered load.
 
\layout Itemize

For the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/\infty$
\end_inset 

 queue the offered load can be much larger than 1 but we know that the utilizati
on factor is never greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

The utilization factor for this queue can be seen to be 
\begin_inset Formula \[
1-P\left(0\right)=1-\exp\left(-a\right).\]

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

